description.narrative:
The project's impact is multi-faceted, addressing immediate and long-term needs of HRD and contributing to the broader landscape of civil society in the region. Key impacts include:
●	The holistic individual safety plans developed under the TSC program enhance the safety of HRDs as a whole, reducing risks;
●	Additional skills and knowledge acquired by beneficiaries as a result of TSC trainings and activities strengthen their professional capacity;
●	Psychological counseling and burnout prevention measures can significantly reduce HRDs' psychological vulnerability and increase their resilience;
●	Interaction between participants from different countries of the program contributes to the development of a network of international professional ties and enhances networking and cooperation;
●	Long-term advocacy impact: by empowering HRDs, they will be able to more effectively promote democratic values and human rights standards in legal crises. 
Overall, the project results have created a positive impact, strengthening the human rights movement in the region and enhancing the safety and effectiveness of HRDs in their ongoing struggle for justice and equality.

●	The TSC conducted 5 regular enrollments, in which a total of 45 human rights defenders (accompanied by a total of 17 family members) from 8 countries participated;
●	At the end of the reporting period, work has begun with the participants of the 18th enrollment;
●	Additionally, 4 emergency cases (accompanied by a total of 4 family members) were supported;
●	8 HRDs have been supported by AD-HOC financial support and emergency psychological counseling;
●	Additionally, in 2023-2024, 8 retreats were held in Ukraine to support a total of 89 Ukrainian HRDs and frontline workers, accompanied by 16 children. This activity was co-funded by the Human Rights House Foundation, OSI, and NED, which enabled us to hold 8 retreats instead of the initially planned 4 within the project budget;
●	TSC organized retreats for 11 and 15 HRDs from Uzbekistan and Kyrgyzstan respectively;
●	25 HRDs from all over the project target region benefited from opportunities to participate in the Advanced online course on security “Ludus Securitas” and 11 HRDs took part in the TOT within this program. These activities have been funded or co-funded by NED, OSI, Human Rights House Foundation and ERIM;
●	Speaking of the Batumi Shelter City, we only use this residence for families with children in need of rest and respite, or for clients with special medical reasons. However, during the reporting period, we did not have any beneficiaries who fit these criteria;
●	At the Telavi Art Residence, we hosted 3 HRDs (from regular enrollments) who were focused on writing books and PhD theses. With support from Protect Defenders.EU, in the second half of 2023, we began repair work on the Residence in order to create a training center. This center will serve for in-house TSC trainings and will also contribute to the sustainability of the organization by being rented out to other NGOs at social prices. Some of the renovation work has begun, but due to the inflexible bureaucratic process, we are awaiting authorization from the Telavi City Hall for additional work at the residence.
